1️⃣ SELECT — choose columns
-- Everything
SELECT * FROM dbo.customers;
SELECT * FROM dbo.orders;

-- Specific columns
SELECT id, country FROM dbo.customers;
SELECT id, country, score FROM dbo.customers;
⚠️ Avoid SELECT * in real code — it pulls every column, breaks when the table changes, and is slower.

2️⃣ WHERE — filter rows (before grouping)
Condition = Expression Operator Expression

Operator type	Operators
Comparison	= <> != > >= < <=
Logical	AND OR NOT
Range	BETWEEN
Membership	IN, NOT IN
Search	LIKE
-- Customers whose score is not zero
SELECT * FROM dbo.customers WHERE score != 0;

-- Exact match (text in single quotes — case usually doesn't matter in SQL Server)
SELECT * FROM dbo.customers WHERE country = 'Germany';

-- Filter first, then pick only 2 columns
SELECT first_name, country
FROM dbo.customers
WHERE country = 'germany';
3️⃣ ORDER BY — sort rows
ASC = ascending (lowest first) — this is the default
DESC = descending (highest first)
Can sort by multiple keys: sorts by the first, ties broken by the second, ...
-- Lowest score first
SELECT * FROM dbo.customers ORDER BY score ASC;

-- Highest score first
SELECT * FROM dbo.customers ORDER BY score DESC;

-- Sort by country A→Z, and within each country highest score first
SELECT * FROM dbo.customers ORDER BY country ASC, score DESC;
4️⃣ GROUP BY — collapse rows into groups + aggregate
Every column in SELECT must be either in GROUP BY or inside an aggregate function (SUM, COUNT, AVG, MIN, MAX).

-- Group by two columns
SELECT first_name, country, SUM(score) AS total
FROM dbo.customers
GROUP BY country, first_name;

-- One row per country: total score + number of customers
SELECT country,
       SUM(score)  AS total_score,
       COUNT(id)   AS no_customers
FROM dbo.customers
GROUP BY country;
5️⃣ HAVING — filter groups (AFTER aggregation)
⚠️ WHERE vs HAVING

WHERE filters rows → runs BEFORE GROUP BY → cannot use aggregates
HAVING filters groups → runs AFTER GROUP BY → CAN use aggregates
-- Countries whose TOTAL score exceeds 800
SELECT country, SUM(score) AS Total_score
FROM dbo.customers
GROUP BY country
HAVING SUM(score) > 800;

-- WHERE + HAVING together: only rows with score > 400,
-- then only groups whose total still exceeds 800
SELECT country, SUM(score) AS Total_score
FROM dbo.customers
WHERE score > 400
GROUP BY country
HAVING SUM(score) > 800;

-- Average score per country (ignoring zeros)
SELECT country, AVG(score) AS Average
FROM dbo.customers
WHERE score != 0
GROUP BY country;

-- ...and keep only countries averaging above 430
SELECT country, AVG(score) AS Average
FROM dbo.customers
WHERE score != 0
GROUP BY country
HAVING AVG(score) > 430;
6️⃣ DISTINCT — remove duplicate values
-- One row per unique country
SELECT DISTINCT country FROM dbo.customers;
DISTINCT applies to the whole selected row combination, not one column. SELECT DISTINCT country, city removes duplicate (country, city) pairs.

7️⃣ TOP — first N rows (SQL Server)
⚠️ TOP without ORDER BY is meaningless — SQL returns arbitrary rows. Always pair TOP with ORDER BY.

-- ⚠️ Arbitrary 3 rows — no order defined
SELECT TOP 3 country, id FROM dbo.customers;
SELECT TOP 3 * FROM dbo.customers;

-- ✅ Top 3 highest scores
SELECT TOP 3 * FROM dbo.customers ORDER BY score DESC;

-- ✅ Bottom 3 scores
SELECT TOP 3 * FROM dbo.customers ORDER BY score ASC;

-- Earliest orders first (TOP works on any sortable column, incl. dates)
SELECT * FROM dbo.orders ORDER BY order_date ASC;
Other databases: MySQL/PostgreSQL use LIMIT n, Oracle uses FETCH FIRST n ROWS ONLY.

8️⃣ Static values (literals) as columns
You can output a fixed value for every row and give it a name with AS:

-- Every row gets a column customer_type = 'new_customer'
SELECT id, first_name, 'new_customer' AS customer_type
FROM dbo.customers;
Note: alias typo (customer_typr) is fixed above — aliases only affect the output column name, not the data.

9️⃣ ⭐ Execution Order vs Coding Order
The single most important thing in this file. You write clauses in one order, but SQL Server executes them in a different order:

CODING order (how you WRITE it):

  SELECT  →  DISTINCT  →  TOP  →  aggregates  →  FROM  →  WHERE  →  GROUP BY  →  HAVING  →  ORDER BY

EXECUTION order (how SQL RUNS it):

  FROM  →  WHERE  →  GROUP BY  →  HAVING  →  SELECT/DISTINCT  →  ORDER BY  →  TOP

  ①  FROM          — get the table
  ②  WHERE         — filter individual rows (no aggregates allowed yet)
  ③  GROUP BY      — collapse rows into groups
  ④  HAVING        — filter the groups (aggregates now available)
  ⑤  SELECT/DISTINCT — compute output columns, drop duplicates
  ⑥  ORDER BY      — sort the final result (can use SELECT aliases here)
  ⑦  TOP           — keep only the first N rows of the sorted result
Why this order explains common errors
Error you hit	Why (execution order)
Can't use aggregate in WHERE (e.g. WHERE SUM(score) > 800)	WHERE runs before GROUP BY — aggregates don't exist yet. Use HAVING.
Can't use a SELECT alias in WHERE	WHERE runs before SELECT — the alias doesn't exist yet.
Can use a SELECT alias in ORDER BY	ORDER BY runs after SELECT — the alias exists by then.
TOP respects ORDER BY	ORDER BY runs before TOP.
📋 Recap Table
Clause	Purpose	Runs	Notes
SELECT	Pick columns / compute output	⑤ (late)	* = all columns; AS renames
FROM	Which table	① (first)	
WHERE	Filter rows	②	No aggregates, no SELECT aliases
GROUP BY	Collapse rows into groups	③	Non-aggregated columns must be listed
HAVING	Filter groups	④	Aggregates allowed
ORDER BY	Sort result	⑥	ASC default, DESC highest first
DISTINCT	Remove duplicate rows	⑤	Applies to full row combination
TOP n	Keep first N rows	⑦ (last)	Always pair with ORDER BY
🃏 Flashcards (flip = answer below)
Q: Filter rows before grouping? → A: WHERE
Q: Filter groups after aggregation? → A: HAVING
Q: WHERE or HAVING — which can use SUM()? → A: HAVING (runs after GROUP BY)
Q: Default sort direction of ORDER BY? → A: ASC (ascending)
Q: Sort by country A→Z and score high→low inside each country? → A: ORDER BY country ASC, score DESC
Q: Remove duplicate values in output? → A: DISTINCT
Q: First 3 highest scores in SQL Server? → A: SELECT TOP 3 * FROM t ORDER BY score DESC
Q: Why is TOP without ORDER BY risky? → A: Rows come back in arbitrary order — you get random rows
Q: First clause SQL executes? → A: FROM (then WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → TOP)
Q: Output a fixed value on every row? → A: SELECT id, 'new_customer' AS customer_type FROM t
Q: Rule for columns in SELECT with GROUP BY? → A: Every column is either in GROUP BY or inside an aggregate
Q: Can ORDER BY use a SELECT alias? Can WHERE? → A: ORDER BY yes, WHERE no (it runs before SELECT)


✅ Practice Checklist
 Select specific columns instead of *
 Filter with WHERE using =, !=, >, AND
 Sort ascending, descending, and by two columns
 Group by one column with SUM and COUNT
 Filter groups with HAVING (e.g. HAVING SUM(score) > 800)
 Combine WHERE + GROUP BY + HAVING in one query
 Get unique country list with DISTINCT
 Get top 3 and bottom 3 with TOP + ORDER BY
 Add a static column with an AS alias
 Recite the execution order from memory: FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT/DISTINCT → ORDER BY → TOP


